From: superterminal <noreply@superterminal.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] gpuix: fix double-lease panic in simulateClick/simulateMouse* on Linux

`GpuixRenderer::simulate_click` (and the other `MouseInput` variants) route
through `UiCommand::DispatchMouse`, which dispatches inside
`WindowHandle::<GpuixView>::update`. That leases `GpuixView` for the whole
dispatch. gpuix then registers a root-level MouseUp handler in
`text::paint::register_drag_listeners`, whose `on_drag_end` closure updates that
same view — a second lease — so *every* synthetic left click panics the
`gpuix-ui` thread:

    thread 'gpuix-ui' panicked at gpui/src/app/entity_map.rs:142:
    cannot update gpuix_native::renderer::GpuixView while it is already being updated

after which every napi call fails with "The GPUI UI thread is not running".

Real compositor input is unaffected: it is dispatched from the platform event
loop without an outer view lease. Only the automation path is broken, which is
why gpuix's own suite (macOS/Windows `TestGpuixRenderer` only) never hits it.

The `UiCommand::DispatchKey` arm immediately below already dispatches through
`gpui::AnyWindowHandle`, which does not lease the view. Do the same for mouse.

Verified on Linux/WSLg with gpuix 0.6.0: 50 `simulateClick` calls on the counter
example take the count 0 -> 50 with no panic; before this change the first click
killed the UI thread. Applies on top of 0001. File upstream and drop this patch
when it lands.
---
--- a/packages/native/src/renderer.rs
+++ b/packages/native/src/renderer.rs
@@ -626,7 +626,13 @@
                 })
             }
             UiCommand::DispatchMouse { input, response } => {
-                let result = window.update(cx, move |_view, window, cx| match input {
+                // `WindowHandle::<GpuixView>::update` leases the view for the whole
+                // dispatch, and gpuix's own root MouseUp handler
+                // (`text::paint::register_drag_listeners` -> `on_drag_end`) updates
+                // that same view -> double-lease panic. Go through the erased handle
+                // instead, exactly as the `DispatchKey` arm below already does.
+                let handle = gpui::AnyWindowHandle::from(window);
+                let result = handle.update(cx, move |_view, window, cx| match input {
                     MouseInput::Click {
                         x,
                         y,
